# MASTER KUBERNETES — 15 Essential Concepts

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| No. | Kubernetes Concept             | Purpose                                                                                                              |
| --- | ------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| 01  | Kubernetes Architecture        | Explains how Kubernetes components work together to deploy and manage containerized applications                     |
| 02  | Control Plane and Worker Nodes | The control plane manages the cluster; worker nodes run application workloads                                        |
| 03  | Pods                           | The smallest deployable units in Kubernetes, containing one or more containers                                       |
| 04  | Namespaces                     | Organize and isolate resources within a Kubernetes cluster                                                           |
| 05  | Deployments                    | Manage application rollouts, updates, and the desired number of replicas                                             |
| 06  | ReplicaSets                    | Maintain a specified number of running Pod replicas                                                                  |
| 07  | Services                       | Provide stable network access to a set of Pods                                                                       |
| 08  | Ingress                        | Manages HTTP/HTTPS access to cluster services through an Ingress controller                                          |
| 09  | Gateway API                    | Provides extensible, role-oriented APIs for managing application traffic routing                                     |
| 10  | ConfigMaps                     | Store non-sensitive application configuration                                                                        |
| 11  | Secrets                        | Store sensitive configuration, such as passwords and tokens; additional security controls are needed to protect them |
| 12  | Persistent Volumes (PV)        | Represent storage resources available to a cluster                                                                   |
| 13  | Persistent Volume Claims (PVC) | Let applications request persistent storage                                                                          |
| 14  | StorageClasses                 | Define storage types and provisioning policies, often enabling dynamic volume creation                               |
| 15  | Requests and Limits            | Configure the CPU and memory resources containers request and are allowed to use                                     |
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

## Recommended learning order ## 

Phase 1 — Understand the cluster : Kubernetes Architecture → Control Plane and Worker Nodes → Pods → Namespaces

Phase 2 — Deploy and expose applications : Deployments → ReplicaSets → Services → Ingress → Gateway API

Phase 3 — Configuration, storage, and resources : ConfigMaps → Secrets → PV → PVC → StorageClasses → Requests and Limits

## Essential `kubectl` commands ##

# Cluster information
kubectl cluster-info
kubectl get nodes

# Pods and namespaces
kubectl get pods
kubectl get pods -A
kubectl get namespaces

# Deployments and services
kubectl create deployment nginx --image=nginx
kubectl get deployments
kubectl scale deployment nginx --replicas=3
kubectl expose deployment nginx --port=80 --type=ClusterIP
kubectl get services

# Inspect and troubleshoot
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>
kubectl logs <pod-name>
kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- sh

# Apply configuration and clean up
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
kubectl delete -f deployment.yaml


Practical goal: Deploy your Dockerized Node.js application to Kubernetes, run multiple replicas,  
                expose it through a Service, configure it using a ConfigMap, and attach persistent storage.

Important distinction: Learn `Ingress` first for traditional HTTP routing, then study the newer `Gateway API`. Gateway API is not simply another name for Ingress; they are distinct Kubernetes networking APIs.
